WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.200
So, widget animations.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.200
It's worth just kind of like breaking this down a little bit.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.199
We've already seen the widget animation window,

4
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:14.199
but it's just worth highlighting that the widget animation system

5
00:00:14.199 --> 00:00:20.199
is essentially a reimplementation of the sequencer.

6
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:22.199
So for anyone who's not familiar with sequencer,

7
00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:25.199
sequencer is Unreal's kind of cinematic authoring tool.

8
00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:28.199
It replaced the previous cinematic authoring tool,

9
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:30.199
which was called Matinee, back in like,

10
00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.490
four-sixteen, I'm going to say.

11
00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.490
Don't quote me on that.

12
00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:40.490
And that, you know, over, it started off as with many things,

13
00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.490
it's an experimental tool and has gained more functionality

14
00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.490
and has become kind of more performant

15
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.490
and more kind of stable over time.

16
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.490
And at some points they introduced that as a,

17
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.490
you didn't used to be able to author widget animations

18
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.490
using sequencer, but that's been introduced.

19
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.490
So when we're talking about widget animations,

20
00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.490
typically we're talking about the implementation,

21
00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.490
the kind of sequencer-esque implementation.

22
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.489
It is not the only way to author animations within a widget.

23
00:01:07.489 --> 00:01:09.489
You can essentially code your animations,

24
00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:12.489
which is actually slightly more performant.

25
00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:16.489
But this is kind of the easiest, most graphical,

26
00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:19.489
most probably user-friendly way to control things.

27
00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:22.489
And it gives us a lot of animation-like control.

28
00:01:22.489 --> 00:01:26.489
So for any animators who want their kind of like nice curves

29
00:01:26.489 --> 00:01:29.489
and to kind of really control all the kind of blends

30
00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.489
and stuff like that, it's quite likely that using

31
00:01:31.489 --> 00:01:33.489
the kind of sequencer implementation of the animation

32
00:01:33.489 --> 00:01:35.489
is really what we're going to be talking about.

33
00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:37.870
So in similar fashion to a sequencer,

34
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.870
each animation is really a collection

35
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:45.870
of kind of named properties or bindings.

36
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.870
So an animation essentially has bindings

37
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.870
to kind of various entities that it animates.

38
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.870
And each of those entities has various properties,

39
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.870
which are kind of defined by individual tracks.

40
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.870
And those tracks can then be keyed.

41
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.870
So keying is a term that will be familiar to anyone

42
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.870
who's used to a little bit of animation before.

43
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.870
And each of these things can be keyframes.

44
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.870
You specify particular values at particular points,

45
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.870
and then some kind of logic will be used

46
00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:19.870
to blend between those two points over several frames.

47
00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.990
Yeah, it's worth mentioning that like particular keyframes

48
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.990
can't be changed at runtime.

49
00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.990
So this is more of a kind of like

50
00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.990
you're basically authoring animations at editor time here.

51
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.990
There are various techniques that you can utilise

52
00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.990
to make these a little bit more dynamic

53
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.990
or possibly reusable and more kind of contextual.

54
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.990
But for the most part, unless you're being a little bit clever

55
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.990
with your blend modes and stuff like that,

56
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.990
you are kind of authoring relatively static information here.

57
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.719
So creating a widget animation,

58
00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:54.719
we've got just a little demonstration

59
00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:56.719
of what a widget animation might look like

60
00:02:56.719 --> 00:03:00.719
in an incredibly high resolution and high frame rate GIF.

61
00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:04.719
But yeah, we're going to do a simple adjustment

62
00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:07.719
of a transform property with an animation.

63
00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:10.719
But obviously the kind of same principles can be layered up

64
00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:12.719
and applied to several animations over time.

65
00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:16.490
So first off, we've got our animation window,

66
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.490
which we've already talked about.

67
00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.490
And this is how you would create a new animation.

68
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.490
You just click the new animation button

69
00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.539
and then you can just name the animation.

70
00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:28.539
And then if we were to go into our animation,

71
00:03:28.539 --> 00:03:31.539
you can see here that when you click on an animation,

72
00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:33.539
the first thing I want to highlight here

73
00:03:33.539 --> 00:03:36.539
is that we get this little perimeter on the edge.

74
00:03:36.539 --> 00:03:40.539
This is basically a warning that lets you know

75
00:03:40.539 --> 00:03:42.539
that you're currently in an animation mode,

76
00:03:42.539 --> 00:03:45.539
which is quite important to bring up

77
00:03:45.539 --> 00:03:48.539
because any changes you're making whilst this is selected

78
00:03:48.539 --> 00:03:50.539
could be things that you're inadvertently keying

79
00:03:50.539 --> 00:03:51.539
into your animation.

80
00:03:51.539 --> 00:03:53.539
And it might not be information

81
00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:55.539
that you're permanently changing about the widget.

82
00:03:55.539 --> 00:03:58.539
So it's just really worth remembering

83
00:03:58.539 --> 00:04:00.539
that when you're in an animation,

84
00:04:00.539 --> 00:04:02.539
you might not be making permanent changes to your widget.

85
00:04:02.539 --> 00:04:05.539
This might be animation that is specifically applying

86
00:04:05.539 --> 00:04:08.539
to that animation.

87
00:04:08.539 --> 00:04:09.539
I'm just going to quickly jump here

88
00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:12.539
because the GIF is incredibly distracting.

89
00:04:12.539 --> 00:04:13.539
If we actually dock that in the layout,

90
00:04:13.539 --> 00:04:16.540
so we make a new animation.

91
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.430
And you can see as soon as I click that,

92
00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.430
I get this border to say, hey, you're in animation mode.

93
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.430
So just try and pay some attention to this.

94
00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.430
Obviously, it's meant to be a little bit obnoxious

95
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.430
to kind of really make sure that you know that.

96
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.430
If you want to be sure that you're not accidentally

97
00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.430
keying information into your animation,

98
00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:38.430
and as importantly, if not more importantly,

99
00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.430
not making the permanent changes

100
00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.430
that you might want to be making to your widget,

101
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.430
just click off the animation

102
00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.430
and you're no longer trying to write information

103
00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.430
into your actual animation.

104
00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:53.259
So then if we were to go into my animation,

105
00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:59.259
I can just basically pick some content to kind of add here.

106
00:04:59.259 --> 00:05:01.259
So I can just go,

107
00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:03.259
if I actually give my images a bit of a name

108
00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:05.259
because this can be a little bit confusing.

109
00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:08.259
This is a general tip that I would say,

110
00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:11.259
it's no different from naming your variables properly

111
00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:13.259
is that for the purposes of animation,

112
00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:15.259
but also for code,

113
00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:17.259
it's kind of good to name your widgets properly

114
00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:19.259
so they can be easily distinguished.

115
00:05:19.259 --> 00:05:22.259
So if I just say like,

116
00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:28.350
I would normally call things like canvas panel underscore roots.

117
00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.350
This is a personal naming convention.

118
00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:33.350
This isn't necessarily something that would be used all over Epic.

119
00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:38.350
But then I'm just going to call this image top left.

120
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:43.579
And I'm going to call this one image center

121
00:05:43.579 --> 00:05:45.579
and just makes them a little bit easier

122
00:05:45.579 --> 00:05:49.180
to distinguish from each other.

123
00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.180
So then if I go to right click,

124
00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.180
you can see I can add,

125
00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.180
this is depending on whatever I've got currently selected.

126
00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.180
You can see I can add a kind of entity

127
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.180
that I want to animate here.

128
00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.180
So this is going to,

129
00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.180
because I currently have image center selected,

130
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.180
I can bind that.

131
00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.180
So if I then do that,

132
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.180
you can see if I click on this plus icon here,

133
00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:13.180
so here's our binding.

134
00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.180
Here's the thing that we're actually trying to animate.

135
00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.180
But then we need to say what kind of like property

136
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.180
about this thing do I want to change?

137
00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.180
Do I want to animate its color and opacity?

138
00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:23.180
Do I want to animate its root slot?

139
00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.180
So for the purpose of moving this thing,

140
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.180
as we've already said,

141
00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.180
the slot is what kind of defines this thing's layout.

142
00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.180
And in the context of a canvas panel,

143
00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.180
the slot has a lot of control over its pixel coordinates

144
00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.180
and stuff like that.

145
00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:37.180
So we'll go and animate the slot.

146
00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.180
And then you see we get this kind of sub track.

147
00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.180
And this is basically to say

148
00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:48.180
this is data that belongs to this particular entity.

149
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:52.180
So if I were to similarly keyframe our image top left

150
00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:55.180
and do the same thing at a canvas slot,

151
00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.180
you can see the kind of way

152
00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.180
that hierarchy is going to be laid out,

153
00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.180
that when I'm talking about this one,

154
00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.180
this is the slot for this image

155
00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:03.180
and this is the slot for this image.

156
00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.180
And similarly, if I were to add something

157
00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.180
that's going to define the color and opacity,

158
00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:11.180
that is the color and opacity for this widget.

159
00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.180
And if I were to add something like, I don't know,

160
00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.180
the visibility track here,

161
00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.180
this is going to be the visibility for this image.

162
00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.180
So these things are kind of like hierarchical.

163
00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:24.180
We have the entity itself

164
00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.180
and then we have the various bits of information

165
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.180
about the entity that we're keyframing.

166
00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.850
So then we just select whatever kind of property

167
00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.850
that we want to keyframe

168
00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.850
and then we just keyframe it essentially.

169
00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.850
The easiest way to probably do this

170
00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.850
is you'll notice here in our details panel,

171
00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.850
we have the kind of various variables available to us

172
00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.850
and you'll notice we've got this little icon

173
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:52.850
that appears on the right.

174
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.850
This essentially represents any bit of information

175
00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:56.850
that is animatable.

176
00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.850
So this isn't just an animation frame thing.

177
00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.850
The same thing appears here.

178
00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.850
You see they're currently kind of grayed out and locked

179
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.850
and that's because we're not in animation.

180
00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:06.850
So keyframing this thing means nothing.

181
00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:09.850
But it's a good way of just taking a quick look

182
00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:12.850
to be like what information would even be keyframable.

183
00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:15.850
This can help when you're kind of designing solutions

184
00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:16.850
to problems to be like,

185
00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:18.850
oh, is it possible that I might be able to use

186
00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.850
an animation to adjust this property?

187
00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.850
If it doesn't have a little keyframe icon next to it,

188
00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:27.850
then know that that variable is not keyframable.

189
00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:28.850
Worth bearing in mind as well

190
00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.850
because it can also answer certain questions for you.

191
00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.850
For example, we have color and opacity of this image

192
00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.850
that is keyframable.

193
00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:37.850
But if we go to our brush,

194
00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.850
our brush's tint isn't keyframable.

195
00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.850
So that can actually lead you to certain conclusions

196
00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:46.850
like if you don't want your code,

197
00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:48.850
if you want to be able to tweak the color of something,

198
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.850
but you want an animation that fades that thing in and out

199
00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:52.850
and you're worried that your code

200
00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.850
is going to clash with your animation,

201
00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.850
bearing in mind that the tint isn't keyframable

202
00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.850
and therefore could never be animated,

203
00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:05.850
maybe the tint is what you can be adjusting the color of

204
00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.850
and you can rely on the fact that color and opacity

205
00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.850
is probably going to be the thing

206
00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.850
that you're adjusting for your opacity.

207
00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:14.850
Although on that particular note,

208
00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.850
there is also a keyframable value,

209
00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.850
which is your render opacity,

210
00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:24.850
which would also specifically keyframe uniquely

211
00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.850
for rendering opacity.

212
00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.850
So maybe you can avoid that problem entirely there

213
00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.620
as well.

214
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.620
So if we were to go to...

215
00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.620
There are several different ways to do this.

216
00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.620
But if...

217
00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:43.620
Sorry, my computer's suddenly running a bit slowly there.

218
00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:48.000
So if we were to add...

219
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.000
Obviously, our Canvas panel slot itself

220
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.000
has several bits of data within it.

221
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.000
So we wouldn't just want to keyframe the slot entirely.

222
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.000
We have lots of different bits of data.

223
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.000
It can be a little bit confusing in the context

224
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:08.059
of our Canvas panel slot

225
00:10:08.059 --> 00:10:11.059
because we have these things called anchors, minimum, maximum,

226
00:10:11.059 --> 00:10:13.059
then we have alignment and offsets,

227
00:10:13.059 --> 00:10:15.059
but we're kind of used to seeing position X and Y.

228
00:10:15.059 --> 00:10:18.059
That's because our position X and Y

229
00:10:18.059 --> 00:10:22.059
is actually an offset from whatever our anchor is.

230
00:10:22.059 --> 00:10:24.059
So when we're talking about our position X and Y,

231
00:10:24.059 --> 00:10:26.059
that's actually this offsets value here.

232
00:10:26.059 --> 00:10:29.059
So if I did offsets and I had a keyframe,

233
00:10:29.059 --> 00:10:32.059
you can see our initial keyframe or initial values

234
00:10:32.059 --> 00:10:34.059
are just whatever our standard values are.

235
00:10:34.059 --> 00:10:35.059
They're not yet keyframes.

236
00:10:35.059 --> 00:10:37.059
So this isn't going to be saved

237
00:10:37.059 --> 00:10:38.059
as the beginning of our animation.

238
00:10:38.059 --> 00:10:41.059
So I can just quickly bind a keyframe here

239
00:10:41.059 --> 00:10:43.059
and you can see I have...

240
00:10:43.059 --> 00:10:47.409
I don't know why those aren't actually...

241
00:10:47.409 --> 00:10:49.409
Oh, they are being written in there just for some reason,

242
00:10:49.409 --> 00:10:52.409
not really appearing properly in my animation.

243
00:10:52.409 --> 00:10:54.409
You can control and mouse wheel, by the way,

244
00:10:54.409 --> 00:10:56.409
to kind of zoom in and out on your timeline.

245
00:10:56.409 --> 00:10:58.409
That's a kind of standard sequencer thing

246
00:10:58.409 --> 00:11:00.409
and right click and then to kind of pan.

247
00:11:00.409 --> 00:11:03.409
And if I were to kind of just drop in

248
00:11:03.409 --> 00:11:05.409
another set of keyframes here,

249
00:11:05.409 --> 00:11:10.139
you can now see I get kind of like

250
00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:12.139
a timeline being constructed

251
00:11:12.139 --> 00:11:14.139
and I can go to these keyframes

252
00:11:14.139 --> 00:11:17.139
and either I can edit my values here.

253
00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:20.139
So I could change this to like 2000

254
00:11:20.139 --> 00:11:22.139
and you would see the widget would move to the right

255
00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:25.139
or actually off the edge of my screen here.

256
00:11:25.139 --> 00:11:27.360
Or I could change that in here.

257
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.360
Now it's worth mentioning that when you're making tweaks

258
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.360
inside the editor itself, this is not currently keyframes.

259
00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.360
Even though I'm over a keyframe,

260
00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.360
I would actually have to choose to bake this change

261
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.360
into my key data by clicking the keyframe button again

